---
title: File & media storage
description: How NeoWorks stores blobs — end-to-end encrypted media, content-addressed and versioned.
---

NeoWorks stores two kinds of data differently. Anything that's just **bytes** — files,
images, video, documents — is treated as media and **encrypted end-to-end**. Structured
records you need to query (contacts, calendar, tasks) live in the [queryable data
layer](/docs/neoworks/openschema) instead.

## How media is stored

Media is encrypted on the client with keys derived from the user's credentials before it is
uploaded. NeoWorks only ever stores the resulting ciphertext — it cannot read your files.

- Keys never leave the user's devices in usable form.
- Blobs are **content-addressed** (addressed by the hash of their ciphertext), so identical
  content is stored once.
- Writes are versioned; previous versions remain retrievable until pruned.

## Uploading

Request the `files:write` scope, then stream the encrypted blob to the storage endpoint. You
receive a content id you can store alongside your structured records.

```http
POST /storage/blobs
Authorization: Bearer <token>
Content-Type: application/octet-stream

<ciphertext>
```

## Downloading

Fetch by content id with the `files:read` scope and decrypt on the client.

```http
GET /storage/blobs/:id
Authorization: Bearer <token>
```

## Limits & billing

Storage counts toward the account's usage. The free tier includes 1&nbsp;GB; paid usage is
billed at cost beyond what's included. See [pricing](/#pricing) for details.

